{
  "image_name": "0cd221797c447c626b13af35d545f5b9.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several visible-light photoredox reactions that proceed through electron donor–acceptor (EDA) complexes. It first illustrates the general concept: a donor and an aryl acceptor associate into a colored EDA complex, and light excitation triggers single-electron transfer to generate a radical ion pair. An energy diagram suggests that this charge-transfer excitation differs from direct excitation of the separate partners.\n\nThe rest of the figure applies this idea to different aryl functionalizations. Heteroaromatic compounds such as pyridines are shown undergoing arylation with either diazonium salts or diaryliodonium salts under blue LEDs, forming biaryl-type products. Anilines also appear to participate in (hetero)arylation through radical intermediates formed after halide loss. Another example shows borylation of arene derivatives, producing an aryl boronic ester from an aryl precursor under light-driven conditions. A final scheme highlights cyanation and alkylation reactions of aryl sulfonium salts, where different conditions divert the same aryl radical intermediate toward nitrile formation or C–C bond formation.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes a common mechanistic theme: preassembled EDA complexes absorb visible light and convert diverse aryl electrophiles into aryl radicals, enabling several synthetically useful bond-forming reactions without an added photocatalyst.",
